# Backend Architecture Rules

This document combines clear architectural guidance with strict enforcement rules to ensure consistent Clean Architecture implementation.

---

# 1. Architectural Style

The backend MUST follow **Clean Architecture principles** with strict separation of layers and inward-facing dependencies.

---

# 2. Layers and Responsibilities

## 2.1 Domain Layer (CORE)

### Responsibilities

* Contains business entities and core business rules
* Defines value objects
* Defines domain errors
* Defines interfaces (contracts) for external dependencies

### Rules

* Must be framework-agnostic
* Must NOT depend on any external framework, ORM, or infrastructure
* Must NOT contain I/O logic (DB, HTTP, filesystem, APIs)
* Must NOT depend on Application, Infrastructure, or Presentation layers
* Entities should be pure and ideally immutable where possible

---

## 2.2 Application Layer (STRICT)

### Responsibilities

* Contains use cases (application services)
* Orchestrates domain entities
* Defines application-specific business workflows
* Implements business logic at the application level

### Rules

* Business logic MUST live in this layer (MANDATORY)
* Must depend ONLY on Domain layer
* Must NOT contain infrastructure logic (DB, HTTP, external APIs)
* Must NOT delegate business rules to a “service layer” outside this layer
* Must NOT be a thin orchestration-only layer (it must contain real business logic)

❌ FORBIDDEN:

* Creating a separate “service layer” for business rules
* Moving business logic into infrastructure services
* Treating Application layer as simple pass-through orchestration only

---

## 2.3 Infrastructure Layer

### Responsibilities

* Implements external systems (databases, APIs, messaging, file storage)
* Provides concrete implementations of interfaces defined in Domain/Application layers

### Rules

* Must NOT contain business logic
* Must NOT define domain rules
* Must depend on Domain/Application ONLY through interfaces
* Must be replaceable without affecting core business logic

---

## 2.4 Presentation Layer

### Responsibilities

* Handles HTTP controllers / API endpoints / transport logic
* Translates requests into Application layer use cases

### Rules

* Must be thin
* Must NOT contain business logic
* Must NOT access infrastructure directly
* Must call Application layer ONLY

❌ FORBIDDEN:

* Implementing business rules in controllers
* Directly interacting with database or external services

---

# 3. Dependency Rule (STRICT)

Dependencies MUST always point inward:

Presentation → Application → Domain
Infrastructure → Domain/Application (via interfaces only)

❌ OUTWARD DEPENDENCY IS FORBIDDEN

---

# 4. Domain Integrity Rule (STRICT)

The Domain layer MUST remain pure and isolated.

❌ FORBIDDEN in Domain:

* Framework dependencies
* Database logic
* HTTP / API logic
* External service calls

✔ Allowed:

* Business rules
* Validation logic
* Domain behaviors

---

# 5. Application Layer Rule (BUSINESS OWNERSHIP)

The Application layer is the **primary home of business logic orchestration and rules**.

* It defines how business processes are executed
* It coordinates domain entities
* It enforces use-case-specific rules

❌ FORBIDDEN:

* Moving business logic into services outside Application layer
* Delegating core decisions to infrastructure

---

# 6. Contracts (Interfaces)

* All external dependencies MUST be abstracted via interfaces
* Interfaces define behavior, not implementation
* Implementations live ONLY in Infrastructure layer

---

# 7. Dependency Injection Rule (STRICT)

* All dependencies MUST be injected (constructor or composition root)
* No direct instantiation of infrastructure inside Domain or Application layers
* No hidden coupling between layers

---

# 8. Summary Principle

> Domain defines the business.
> Application executes the business.
> Infrastructure supports the business.
> Presentation exposes the business.

Everything else is a violation of architecture boundaries.
